---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The Brother"
  eyebrow="Oto Blažević, 1923–1945"
  dek="Hedviga's brother died at twenty-two in the last year of the war. The family preserves two accounts of how, they cannot both be true, and there is no grave to ask.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This page exists because the record contains a
    <strong>contradiction that has been kept rather than tidied away</strong>. Both accounts are
    written into the same note on Oto's entry, attributed to two different people, and this archive
    reproduces them exactly as they stand.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The first account</h3>
        <blockquote class="note">Bitten by snake<span class="dim"> — attributed in the tree to
          <strong>Ivan Defranceski</strong></span></blockquote>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">Ivan Anton Defranceski (1925–1995) was Hedviga's husband and
        Oto's brother-in-law — two years younger than Oto, and alive in the same town at the time.
        The Velebit has the <strong>horned viper</strong>, <em>Vipera ammodytes</em>, Europe's most
        dangerous snake, and a shepherd or a labourer on that mountain was genuinely at risk.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The second account</h3>
        <blockquote class="note">The late Otto Blažević was the brother of Ljuba, Leo, Aunt Karma,
        Aunt Seka … he died in the war somewhere in <strong>Slavonia</strong>, his grave is unknown,
        so they put his name in his grandmother's and grandfather's grave — and now Leo is there,
        and Katica.<span class="dim"> — attributed in the tree to
        <strong>Nives Blažević</strong></span></blockquote>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">This one names his siblings correctly, including
        <strong>“Aunt Seka”</strong> — Hedviga's own nickname — which is a good sign that the
        speaker knew the family well.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the second account fits</h2>
    <p class="lede">The war in Slavonia in <strong>1945</strong> is not a vague setting. It is one
    of the best-documented killing grounds of the European war's last months.</p>
    <p>The <strong>Syrmian Front</strong> ran from <strong>21 October 1944 to 12 April 1945</strong>
    across Syrmia, <strong>Slavonia</strong> and Baranja. The Yugoslav Partisans lost
    <strong>more than 13,500 killed</strong> covering roughly 140 kilometres between Belgrade and
    Vinkovci over nearly six months of winter fighting. When the front broke on 12 April, the 1st
    and 3rd Armies advanced <strong>west through Slavonia</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">He had already seen the war close up. He was
    <strong>twenty</strong> when <a href={u("/senj-1943")}>German aircraft destroyed about half of
    Senj on 7–8 October 1943</a>, killing thirty of the town's residents — among them, on the
    second day, his uncle by marriage <strong>Pavao Kosina</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">A man born in 1923 was <strong>twenty-one or twenty-two</strong> in
    that campaign — precisely the cohort mobilised into it, much of it from newly-liberated Croatia
    with minimal training. And an unknown grave is the ordinary outcome, not the strange one:
    thousands of those dead were buried where they fell.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>The detail that rings true.</strong> That the family
    <em>cut his name into his grandparents' stone</em> because there was no body is a specific,
    unglamorous, expensive act. People do it when someone does not come back from somewhere far
    away. It is much harder to explain if he died of a snakebite on the hill above the town.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">New evidence, September 2026</div>
    <h2>The grave says she was right about the grave</h2>
    <p class="lede">A photograph of the <a href={u("/grave")}>Blažević plot at Senj</a> — recovered
    from the MyHeritage media library, where it had been sitting untouched — can be checked against
    Nives Blažević's account line by line. <strong>It matches.</strong></p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4503429-blazevic-grave.jpg")}
           alt="The Blažević family grave at Senj, with Oto's name on it" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>LJUBOMIR BLAŽEVIĆ ✳1891 †1966 · MILKA BLAŽEVIĆ <em>rod.</em> PAPIĆ ✳1886 †1971 ·
      <strong>OTO BLAŽEVIĆ ✳1923 †1945</strong> · MARIO BLAŽEVIĆ ✳1933 †1984 — and, on a later
      plaque, KATA ✳1924 †2007 and LEO ✳1920 †2008.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <tr><th>She said</th><td>“they put his name in … [the] grave”</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The stone</th><td><strong>OTO BLAŽEVIĆ ✳ 1923 † 1945</strong> is cut into it, between
        his mother and his brother Mario.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>She said</th><td>“and now <strong>Leo</strong> is there, and <strong>Katica</strong>”</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The stone</th><td>A separate, later, black plaque below reads
        <strong>LEO ✳ 1920 † 2008</strong> and <strong>KATA ✳ 1924 † 2007</strong> — Katica.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>She said</th><td>“his grandmother's and grandfather's grave”</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The stone</th><td>The names are Oto's <em>parents</em>. But
        <strong>Nives Blažević was born in 1953</strong> — Ljubomir and Milka were
        <em>her</em> grandparents. She is describing the grave from where she stands in the family,
        which is what people do.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>What this does and does not settle.</strong> It does
    <em>not</em> show that Oto died in Slavonia. It shows that the witness who said so
    <strong>knew this grave in exact detail</strong>, including two burials added more than sixty
    years after his death — and that the family commemorated him on a stone rather than at a grave
    of his own, which is what you do for a man who does not come back.
    <strong>The snakebite account now sits second.</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How this archive leaves it</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Documented</th><td>Born 1923, Senj. Died 1945, with the death place entered in the
        tree as <strong>Senj</strong>. Son of Ljubomir Blažević and Milka Papić; brother of Leo,
        Karmela, Hedviga, Ljubomir, Mario and Anton.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Family lore, first</th><td>Snakebite. Attributed to Ivan Defranceski, who was
        there.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Family lore, second</th><td>Killed in the war in Slavonia, no known grave, name added
        to his grandparents' plot. Attributed to Nives Blažević.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Corroborated</th><td>Nives's account, in its checkable parts: his name on the family
        stone, and Leo and Katica added to it later. <a href={u("/grave")}>The grave →</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Contradiction</th><td><strong>Still unresolved as to cause.</strong> The tree's own
        <em>death place</em> of Senj agrees with neither account cleanly — it contradicts Slavonia
        outright, and it is also exactly what a family would enter when the real place was
        unknown.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>What would settle it.</strong> The Croatian
    State Archives hold Second World War military and civil death records, and the
    <em>Yugoslav war victims register</em> (<em>Popis žrtava rata</em>) is searchable by name and
    birth year. A single entry for <strong>Oto Blažević, b. 1923, Senj</strong> would end the
    question in either direction. Nobody has yet looked.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Both accounts are kept because pretending otherwise is
    how family myths become family history — and because one of these two people was right.
    <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
